We have method set(int index, RoleUnresolved role). It calls super.set(index, role). The comment: "If the index is within bounds and the role is not null, then for every index i less than the specified index, the element at i remains unchanged if it was not null before the operation."

We need to translate to formal assertion.

Premise: index is within bounds and role != null. Since method throws IllegalArgumentException if role null, and IndexOutOfBoundsException if index out of range, but we can express premise: (0 <= index && index < this.size()) && role != null.

Then conclusion: for every int i; 0 <= i && i < index; if \old(this.get(i)) != null then this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))). Actually "remains unchanged if it was not null before the operation." Means if old element at i was not null, then after operation it's equal to old element. If old element was null, maybe could become something else? Not required. So spec: \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; (\old(this.get(i)) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))).

We need to refer to role not null? Already premise includes role != null.

Thus final assert: assert (0 <= index && index < this.size() && role != null) => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; (\old(this.get(i)) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)));

But need to ensure parentheses for implication: (premise) => (forall...). Use parentheses.

